## Blinking text and rotating image

Two CSS animations are used. One changes opacity for blinking, one rotates for spinning.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<title>Blinking Text and Rotating Image</title>
<style>
#blink {
 color: blue;
 font-size: 20px;
 animation: blink 2s infinite;
}
@keyframes blink {
 0% { opacity: 1; }
 50% { opacity: 0; }
 100% { opacity: 1; }
}
#spin {
 width: 100px;
 height: 100px;
 background-color: blue;
 color: white;
 animation: spin 3s infinite linear;
}
@keyframes spin {
 from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
 to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<p id="blink">This text blinks blue!</p>
<div id="spin">Blue text in an image</div>
</body>
</html>
```

## How it works

1. Blink moves opacity from visible to vanished and back every two seconds.
2. Spin moves rotation from 0 to 360 degrees, waits through timing and repeats.
3. Style holds the rules and keyframes defines each stage.

## Sum program with ordered list

The numbers are shown as a numbered list by using ol and li created from the array.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Sum of First 10 Integers</title>
</head>
<body>
<button onclick="calculateSum()">Calculate Sum</button>
<ol id="numberList"></ol>
<p id="sumDisplay"></p>
<script>
function calculateSum() {
 let sum = 0;
 let numbers = [];
 for (let i = 1; i <= 10; i++) {
  sum += i;
  numbers.push(i);
 }
 const list = document.getElementById("numberList");
 list.innerHTML = "";
 for (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
  const li = document.createElement("li");
  li.textContent = numbers[i];
  list.appendChild(li);
 }
 document.getElementById("sumDisplay").textContent = "The sum is " + sum;
}
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

## Change explained

1. Ul is replaced by ol so bullets become numbers 1, 2, 3.
2. Each array value becomes one li through the loop.
3. Push still appends each integer, and sum still adds each value.

## Mixed array program

JavaScript allows words, numbers and boolean values in one array.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<title>Mixed Array</title>
</head>
<body>
<p id="output"></p>
<script>
const My_Mix_Array = ["apple", 3.14, true, "Ali", 25];
let text = "My_Mix_Array : [ ";
for (let i = 0; i < My_Mix_Array.length; i++) {
 text += My_Mix_Array[i];
 if (i < My_Mix_Array.length - 1) {
  text += ", ";
 }
}
text += " ]";
document.getElementById("output").textContent = text;
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

## Result

The page shows: My_Mix_Array : [ apple, 3.14, true, Ali, 25 ]. Apple is at index 0 and 25 is at index 4.

## Search with iteration count

The list is searched from start to end and a counter records how many checks were made.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<title>Search Count</title>
</head>
<body>
<p id="list"></p>
<p id="result"></p>
<script>
const shoppingList = ["milk", "bread", "eggs", "apples", "cheese", "butter", "sugar", "tea", "rice", "salt"];
function findWithCount(itemName) {
 let count = 0;
 for (let i = 0; i < shoppingList.length; i++) {
  count++;
  if (shoppingList[i] === itemName) {
   return itemName + " found after " + count + " iterations.";
  }
 }
 return itemName + " not found after " + count + " iterations.";
}
document.getElementById("list").textContent = "List: " + shoppingList.toString();
document.getElementById("result").textContent = findWithCount("milk") + " " + findWithCount("honey");
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

## How count works

1. Loop checks each item in order until a match is found.
2. If found early, count equals its position plus one.
3. If absent, the loop finishes all 10 checks, so count is 10.
